.TH IO_WAITUNTIL2 3 "c-utils" "C Utility Functions"
.SH NAME
io_waituntil2 \- wait for I/O events on registered file descriptors with millisecond timeout
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <io.h>
.PP
.BI "int64 io_waituntil2(int64 " milliseconds ");"

.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR io_waituntil2 ()
waits for I/O events (readability or writability) on file descriptors registered with the
.B io
library, or until a timeout specified in milliseconds has elapsed.

The
.I milliseconds
argument specifies the maximum duration to wait:
.TP
.B \-1
Block indefinitely until at least one registered file descriptor becomes ready.
.TP
.B 0
Return immediately after checking for pending I/O events without blocking.
.TP
.B > 0
Wait up to
.I milliseconds
milliseconds for I/O activity.

If no file descriptors are currently registered for reading or writing (\fBio_wanted_fds\fR is 0),
.BR io_waituntil2 ()
returns 0 immediately without making multiplexing system calls.

When an event occurs on a file descriptor,
.BR io_waituntil2 ()
updates the internal descriptor state (\fBcanread\fR / \fBcanwrite\fR flags) and prepends ready descriptors
to the internal linked lists (\fBfirst_readable\fR and \fBfirst_writeable\fR) so that subsequent calls to
.BR io_canread ()
and
.BR io_canwrite ()
can retrieve them.

.SH MULTIPLEXING BACKENDS
Depending on build configuration and operating system support,
.BR io_waituntil2 ()
transparently utilizes one of several multiplexing backends:
.IP \(bu 3
.B Linux epoll
(\fBEPOLL\fR): Uses \fBepoll_wait\fR(2) with lazy kernel event updates via \fBepoll_ctl\fR(2).
.IP \(bu 3
.B BSD kqueue
(\fBKQUEUE\fR): Uses \fBkevent\fR(2).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Solaris /dev/poll
(\fBDEVPOLL\fR): Uses \fBioctl\fR(2) with \fBDP_POLL\fR.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Windows I/O Completion Ports
(\fBCOMPLETIONPORT\fR / \fBGetQueuedCompletionStatus\fR): Handles asynchronous overlapped read, write, connect, accept, and sendfile operations natively.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Real-time Signals
(\fB_SIGIO\fR): Uses \fBsigtimedwait\fR(2) / \fBsigwaitinfo\fR(2).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Linux AIO
(\fBUSE_LINUX_AIO\fR): Uses kernel AIO system calls (\fBio_submit\fR(2), \fBio_getevents\fR(2)).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Portable select / poll
(\fBUSE_SELECT\fR / \fBHAVE_POLL\fR): Uses \fBselect\fR(2) or \fBpoll\fR(2) as a fallback mechanism.

.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR io_waituntil2 ()
returns:
.TP
.B >= 1
The number of ready events or file descriptors processed.
.TP
.B 0
No descriptors were active, no events occurred, or the timeout elapsed.
.TP
.B \-1
An error occurred during the multiplexing system call (e.g., \fBpoll\fR(2), \fBselect\fR(2), or \fBepoll_wait\fR(2) failed), and \fBerrno\fR is set appropriately.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR io_wait (3),
.BR io_wantread (3),
.BR io_wantwrite (3),
.BR io_canread (3),
.BR io_canwrite (3),
.BR epoll_wait (2),
.BR poll (2),
.BR select (2),
.BR kevent (2)